<p>Everything an operator does happens under <strong>Administration</strong> (<code>/admin</code>). The menu is divided into sections; which entries an account sees depends on its roles (see the chapter "Accounts, roles and permissions"). Under <strong>Profile settings → Layout of the administration</strong>, the same menu can be shown as a bar on top instead.</p>

<p>Every page of the administration works the same way: the form on it is sent as a whole, a flash message above the content says what happened, and where a list is long it has pages. A row or a card with a link goes to its detail page.</p>

<h2>The dashboard</h2>
<p>The start page shows one card per area the account may manage: the number of items, and what waits for a decision. The "Updates" card shows how many components have a newer release. Below the cards are all areas as links.</p>

<h2>The sections at a glance</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Section</th><th scope="col">What happens there</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Content</td><td>Pages (with editing the home page in place), media library, offer page, menu, widgets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marketplace</td><td>Offers, categories, custom fields, providers, requests, orders, withdrawals, payment methods</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moderation</td><td>Reviews, account ratings, reports, messages, word filter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>People</td><td>Accounts, roles</td></tr>
        <tr><td>System</td><td>Settings, translations, design, modules, themes, packages, extensions, shop, demo data, backup, export/import, tasks, updates</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Help</td><td>Handbook, documentation, credits, support</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>The "Marketplace" section only appears while an app extension that brings a catalogue is running; the same goes for entries such as offers or categories. An enabled addon can add a menu entry of its own, such as "Subscriptions" (only with <code>modulento-ext-abos</code>) or "FAQ" - addons with no group of their own end up under "More".</p>

<h2>Shop and support: bought extensions and themes</h2>
<p><strong>Administration → Shop</strong> shows what the configured license server sells, and buys a product right there through Stripe - the license key that produces lands under <strong>Administration → Extensions</strong> on its own, no copying a key by hand. A master license (one key valid for every product the server sells) skips Stripe entirely: every product then shows "Install" instead of "Buy".</p>
<p>For any product this installation holds a license key for, <strong>Administration → Support</strong> opens a ticket to the license server's operator - no separate account there, authenticated with that same key. With no license server configured, or no key stored yet, the page says so plainly instead of showing an empty form. An image, PDF or text file up to 2 MB can be attached when opening or replying to a ticket; a new reply from the operator eventually also shows up as a notification at the bell icon up top.</p>

<h2>Switching modules on and off</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Modules</strong>, a built-in function of the core can be switched off without losing its data: reviews, the contact form, withdrawals, reports, avatars, "stay signed in", the inbox, notifications and requests. Off means: no routes, no menu entry, no link to it anywhere - the tables stay, and switching it back on shows its old data at once. An extension can add a module of its own here.</p>

<h2>Permissions decide the menu, not roles by name</h2>
<p>What an account sees in the menu follows its permissions, not its name. An account without <code>core.admin.access</code> never sees the administration at all, whatever else it may do. More on this in the chapter "Accounts, roles and permissions".</p>
